AU  - Sanjaya, Mohammad Ovi
AU  - Djunaidy, Arif
KW  - Training;Measurement;Procedural generation;Correlation;Reinforcement learning;Maintenance enginee...
AB  - This paper proposes an adaptive level-generation framework that integrates Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) with the Token-based One-shot Arbitrary Dimension GAN (TOAD-GAN) to achieve dynamic difficulty adjustment in platformer games. The PPO agent maps player-behavior metrics to difficulty parameters that condition TOAD-GAN through a post-generation imputation module. The approach avoids retraining the generator by modifying enemy density, power-up distribution, and time limits directly on the tokenized output. Experiments show that PPO trained with a difficulty-margin simulator converges substantially better than a random simulator baseline (average reward −136.7 vs. −2260.8). Structural fidelity is preserved, with TPKL-Divergence increasing only modestly (0.47 → 0.57 after difficulty imputation). A rule-based playability repair module further improves solvability from 65% to 80% across 100 generated levels. Results demonstrate that RL-driven conditioning can effectively guide token-based generators toward producing adaptive and playable content.
